## Why This Is Asked
This question tests lifecycle design for prompt templates, including versioning, canary deployments, per-tenant experiments, and safe rollback, all critical in production chat systems for high-safety domains.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned, tenant-scoped templates
- Canary rollouts and drift monitoring
- Safe rollback via veto gates and audit logs
- Data model: Tenant, TemplateVersion, Deployment, Experiment, RollbackLog

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype: tenant -> version
const registry = {
  'tenantA': { current: 'v2', canary: 'v3' },
};

function resolveLatestVersion(tenant, canary = false) {
  const t = registry[tenant] || { current: null };
  return canary && t.canary ? t.canary : t.current;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure drift in template performance and safety?
- How would you implement per-tenant experiments without latency penalties?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to enforce tenancy isolation and data minimization in prompts and outputs, plus post-checks before delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented prompts, tenancy scoping, redaction, post-filtering, test harness concepts.

## Code Example
```javascript
function wrapPrompt(tenantId, userPrompt) {
  // basic tenant-scoped redaction and prefix
  const safePrompt = `[TENANT:${tenantId}] PROMPT: ${userPrompt}`;
  return safePrompt;
}

function mockLLM(prompt){
  // simulate a model that may leak data
  return `Response for ${prompt} with tenant id ${prompt.match(/TENANT:(\\w+)/)[1]}`;
}

function postCheck(response, tenantId){
  // naive leakage check
  if (response.includes(tenantId)) return null;
  return response;
}

console.log(postCheck(mockLLM(wrapPrompt('T123','List logs for user emails')),
  'T123'));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve this for real-world tenancy boundaries and performance at scale?
- What tests would you add to catch redaction misses and leakage?
- How would you handle dynamic redaction rules per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical, beginner-friendly approach to prompt routing with real constraints like ownership and cost, rather than abstract theory.

## Key Concepts
- Rule-based routing with explicit signals
- Multi-tenant data access considerations
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, data-source mix, missing ranges

## Code Example
```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, signals){
  // simple scoring demo
  const modules = ['SQL-Query-Builder','Ownership-Policy-Checker','Visualization-Generator'];
  // dummy scoring based on intent match
  const scores = modules.map(m => {
    if(m==='SQL-Query-Builder' && signals.intent.includes('top') ) return 2;
    if(m==='Ownership-Policy-Checker' && signals.data_ownership==='prod') return 2;
    return 1;
  });
  const idx = scores.indexOf(Math.max(...scores));
  return {module: modules[idx], payload: {query:'SELECT region, SUM(revenue) FROM sales WHERE ...', limit:5}};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to dynamic cost models and per-user quotas?
- How would you test routing decisions with synthetic prompts and ensure no data leakage?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to translate policy into a concrete routing mechanism with auditability for multi-tenant enterprise chat systems.

## Key Concepts

- Signals: data-sensitivity, intent, policy-violations
- Modes: taboo-free, context-constrained, escalate
- Validation: unit tests with synthetic prompts and edge cases

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype
function analyze(p){
  return { policyViolation: false, redacted: false };
}
function routePrompt(p){
  const s = analyze(p);
  if (s.policyViolation) return { mode: 'escalate', reason: 'policy' };
  if (s.redacted) return { mode: 'context-constrained', reason: 'redaction' };
  return { mode: 'taboo-free' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you log decisions for audits without leaking PII?
- How would you detect drift in routing quality over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Aims to gauge handling of ambiguous prompts with simple, maintainable logic—crucial for multi-tenant data workflows without heavy ML.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight keyword-based intent detection
- Ambiguity handling with clarifying questions
- Structured 2-step plans and module payloads
- Lightweight testing with sample prompts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo JS prototype for disambiguation harness
function detectIntents(text){
  const intents = {
    time: /(last|recent|past|last month)/i,
    region: /(region|country|geo)/i,
    users: /(users|accounts|people)/i
  };
  const hits = Object.keys(intents).filter(k => intents[k].test(text));
  return hits;
}

function disambiguate(text){
  const intents = detectIntents(text);
  if (intents.length > 1){
    return {ambiguous: true, options: ["summarize by region", "summarize by time"]};
  }
  if (intents.length === 1){
    return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Fetch data", "Summarize"], payload: {module: intents[0]}};
  }
  return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Interpret", "Respond"], payload: {module: 'default'}};
}

console.log(disambiguate("summarize recent activity"));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the heuristic to handle multilingual prompts?
- What tests would you add to prevent regression when adding new intents?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to architect provenance-aware prompt pipelines at scale, ensuring data lineage, watermarking, and auditability across tenants with realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and lineage in LLM prompts
- Per-tenant guards, watermarking, policy enforcement
- Output verification with source citations via a mock knowledge graph
- Structured audit logging: input hash, rewritten prompt, per-model outputs, latency, drift verdict
- Security considerations around prompt leakage and PII

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function rewriteWithProvenance(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  const provenance = `Source=${tenantPolicy.allowedSources.join('|')};Tenant=${tenantPolicy.id}`;
  return `${prompt}\n\n[Provenance:${provenance}]`;
}
function verifyOutput(output, sources) {
  const hasCitation = sources.some(s => output.includes(s));
  return hasCitation;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle non-determinism across models while preserving provenance guarantees?
- How would you extend the audit log to support compliance reviews and exception handling?
